            <ArticleTitle>A case report on a rare anatomic variant of cystic duct insertion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>rjx131</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/jscr/rjx131</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a case a 70-year-old female presenting with abdominal pain who has a cystic duct coursing medial to the common bile duct before anastomosing just proximal to the ampulla. Low medial insertion of the cystic duct is a rare anatomic variant that is easily misidentified on imaging studies and may complicate surgical and percutaneous intervention. It can be identified by pre-operative imaging modalities such as magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
